      "id": "M4171-A",
      "skill": "engineering-application-journal-writing-skill",
      "language": "en",
      "prompt": "Request A: An unsigned unit-weight hypergraph on three nodes has either one hyperedge {1,2,3} (case A), or three hyperedges {1,2},{1,3},{2,3} (case B). In this supplied illustration the propagation matrix is S=Dv^(-1/2) H De^(-1) H^T Dv^(-1/2), where H is binary node-by-hyperedge incidence, De has hyperedge cardinalities, and Dv has node incidence counts. All degrees are positive. Compute the incidence shapes, binary pairwise adjacency with diagonal removed, and S times x=(1,0,0)^T for each case. Do identical binary projected graphs imply identical incidence structure or this specified propagation? Can an ordinary weighted graph/matrix use the same S for this particular layer, and does the example prove universal expressivity or fault-diagnosis superiority? Separately, explain why changing node features, recomputing a sample-neighborhood graph for each permitted batch, and learning model parameters online are different changing objects; a declared transductive unlabeled batch is not automatically a forbidden information source. Do not infer physical variable relations if the actual nodes are samples. Inspect whether the Skill has a completed matched hypergraph/hypergraph-convolution definition record and separate Chinese wordings, without treating absence as invalidity. ",
